    {
      "article_id": "article_1",
      "article_number": 1,
      "title_original": "/ p$, and there",
      "title_safe": "SLASH p and there",
      "page_start": 278,
      "page_end": 278,
      "content": {
        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 278 ---\n154.]\nCYLINDERS AND PARABOLOIDS.\n243\nWhen $b=c$, we may take\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\alpha=-\\int_{0}^{\\lambda} \\frac{d \\lambda}{b-\\lambda} \\\\\n& \\gamma=\\int_{2 b}^{\\nu} \\frac{d \\nu}{\\nu-b} \\\\\n& \\lambda=\\left\\{b 1-e^{\\alpha}\\right\\} \\\\\n& \\nu=b\\left\\{1+e^{\\gamma}\\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}$$\nFrom (51)\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& (\\mu-b)=\\frac{1}{2}(c-b)\\{1-\\cos \\beta\\}, \\\\\n& (c-\\mu)=\\frac{1}{2}(c-b)\\{1+\\cos \\beta\\} ;\n\\end{aligned}$$\nhence from (50),\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nx & =b+b\\left(e^{\\gamma}-e^{a}\\right), \\\\\ny^{2} & =4 b^{2} e^{\\gamma+a} \\sin ^{2} \\frac{\\beta}{2}, \\\\\nz^{2} & =4 b^{2} e^{\\gamma+a} \\cos ^{2} \\frac{\\beta}{2} .\n\\end{aligned}$$\nIf we take the origin at the focus $x=b$, and write $2 \\beta^{\\prime}$ for $\\beta, a e^{2 \\gamma^{\\prime}}$ for $b \\epsilon \\gamma, a \\epsilon^{2 \\beta}$\nfor $b \\epsilon^{a}$, we get\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& x=e^{2 \\gamma^{\\prime}}-e 2^{a^{\\prime}}, \\\\\n& y=2 a e^{a^{\\prime}+\\gamma^{\\prime}} \\sin \\beta^{\\prime} \\\\\n& z=2 a \\epsilon^{a^{\\prime}+\\gamma^{\\prime}} \\cos \\beta^{\\prime} .\n\\end{aligned}$$\nFrom which equations of the form (54) may easily be deduced.\nSince from these equations the force along the radius varies as $1 / r$, the normal force,\nand therefore the surface-density, will vary as $\\frac{1}{r} \\cdot \\frac{r}{p}$ where $p$ is the perpendicular\nfrom the focus on the tangent plane, thus the surface-density varies as $1 / p$, and there-\nfore inversely as the square root of $r$. \\}\n",
        "mathpix_markdown": "\n--- Page 278 ---\n\n\nWhen $b=c$, we may take\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\alpha=-\\int_{0}^{\\lambda} \\frac{d \\lambda}{b-\\lambda} \\\\\n& \\gamma=\\int_{2 b}^{\\nu} \\frac{d \\nu}{\\nu-b} \\\\\n& \\lambda=\\left\\{b 1-e^{\\alpha}\\right\\} \\\\\n& \\nu=b\\left\\{1+e^{\\gamma}\\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n\nFrom (51)\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& (\\mu-b)=\\frac{1}{2}(c-b)\\{1-\\cos \\beta\\}, \\\\\n& (c-\\mu)=\\frac{1}{2}(c-b)\\{1+\\cos \\beta\\} ;\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nhence from (50),\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nx & =b+b\\left(e^{\\gamma}-e^{a}\\right), \\\\\ny^{2} & =4 b^{2} e^{\\gamma+a} \\sin ^{2} \\frac{\\beta}{2}, \\\\\nz^{2} & =4 b^{2} e^{\\gamma+a} \\cos ^{2} \\frac{\\beta}{2} .\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n\nIf we take the origin at the focus $x=b$, and write $2 \\beta^{\\prime}$ for $\\beta, a e^{2 \\gamma^{\\prime}}$ for $b \\epsilon \\gamma, a \\epsilon^{2 \\beta}$\n for $b \\epsilon^{a}$, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& x=e^{2 \\gamma^{\\prime}}-e 2^{a^{\\prime}}, \\\\\n& y=2 a e^{a^{\\prime}+\\gamma^{\\prime}} \\sin \\beta^{\\prime} \\\\\n& z=2 a \\epsilon^{a^{\\prime}+\\gamma^{\\prime}} \\cos \\beta^{\\prime} .\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n\nFrom which equations of the form (54) may easily be deduced.\n\nSince from these equations the force along the radius varies as $1 / r$, the normal force,\n and therefore the surface-density, will vary as $\\frac{1}{r} \\cdot \\frac{r}{p}$ where $p$ is the perpendicular\n from the focus on the tangent plane, thus the surface-density varies as $1 / p$, and there\nfore inversely as the square root of $r$. \\}\n",
